# Introduction

Monte Carlo methods offer a powerful and flexible approach to numerical integration and simulation, especially in high-dimensional or complex spaces where analytical solutions are challenging to derive. In this task, we will explore Monte Carlo integration and simulation through two problems:

We will approximate the integral of an exponential function using uniformly distributed random variables for a specified finite interval and compare it to its analytical value. We will also extend this calculation to an infinite interval, considering an appropriate probability density function for the simulation. This comparison will highlight when and why Monte Carlo methods yield accurate estimates relative to classical integration techniques.

In this part, we will use Monte Carlo simulation to estimate the area enclosed by a polar curve. By generating random points in a bounding rectangle and evaluating whether they fall within the enclosed region, we can approximate the area. This simulation will be performed at different levels of granularity (100 to 100,000 points), showcasing the convergence behavior of Monte Carlo estimates. We will visualize the function and estimate the enclosed area, summarizing results in a table and plots.

Through these tasks, we will illustrate how Monte Carlo techniques can provide numerical solutions to complex integrals and area estimations using random sampling, along with an analysis of their accuracy and convergence behavior.

