### 1.2 Use Monte Carlo integration to compute the integral for b = ∞. What would be a good density for the simulation in that case? Use also the function integrate for comparison.

In this task, we extend our previous Monte Carlo integration approach to approximate the value of the integral 

$$
\int_{0}^{6} e^{-x^3} dx.
$$

Since the integration interval is infinite, using a uniform distribution is inefficient. Instead, we will select an appropriate probability density function (PDF) to better simulate the behavior of the integrand and achieve accurate results. Additionally, we will compare our Monte Carlo estimate with the exact value obtained using a numerical integration function to evaluate the accuracy of our approach for integrals over infinite domains.

``` {r task1.2}
integrand <- function(x) {
  tryCatch({
    exp(-x^3)
  }, error = function(e) {
    stop("Error in integrand function: ", e$message)
  })
}

monte_carlo_integration_inf <- function(n_samples) {
  if (!is.numeric(n_samples) || n_samples <= 0) {
    stop("Error: n_samples must be a positive integer.")
  }
  
  samples <- tryCatch({
    rexp(n_samples, 1)
  }, error = function(e) {
    stop("Error generating samples: ", e$message)
  })
  
  tryCatch({
    mean(integrand(samples) * exp(samples))
  }, error = function(e) {
    stop("Error in Monte Carlo integration: ", e$message)
  })
}

n_samples <- 10000

monte_carlo_result <- tryCatch({
  monte_carlo_integration_inf(n_samples)
}, error = function(e) {
  stop("Error calculating Monte Carlo result: ", e$message)
})

exact_value <- tryCatch({
  integrate(integrand, 0, Inf)$value
}, error = function(e) {
  stop("Error calculating exact value: ", e$message)
})

cat("Monte Carlo Estimate for b=Inf:", monte_carlo_result, "\n")
cat("Exact Value for b=Inf:", exact_value, "\n")
```
The Monte Carlo estimate for the integral over b=∞ is approximately 0.893, and the exact value calculated using numerical integration is 0.893 as well. The two values are very close, indicating that the Monte Carlo integration method worked effectively for this problem.

In this approach, using an exponential distribution with a rate parameter of 1 as the sampling density was a good choice because it decays at a similar rate as the integrand. This helps to ensure efficient sampling without an excessive number of outlier values, contributing to a low-variance estimate that converges to the correct value quickly. The close match between the Monte Carlo estimate and the exact value demonstrates the validity of using this density for importance sampling in this context.

### 1.3 Do you have an explanation why Monte Carlo integration agrees in 2. with integrate but not so much in 1.?

The better agreement for b=∞ occurs because Monte Carlo integration used an exponential distribution that closely matches the decay of the integrand, leading to more efficient sampling and lower variance. In contrast, for b=6, using a uniform distribution resulted in less efficient sampling, with higher variance, since many samples fall in regions where the integrand contributes less, reducing precision.

