### 2.2 Generate uniform random coordinates within the rectangle [−2,3.5]×[−3,3] and an indicator whether this point lies within the area in question.

In this task, we will use Monte Carlo simulation to estimate the area enclosed by a polar curve. To do so, we first generate uniform random coordinates within a defined rectangular region [−2,3.5]×[−3,3], which acts as our bounding box. For each random point generated, we will determine whether it lies inside the area enclosed by the curve. This is done by comparing the point's distance from the origin to the value of the function r(t) that defines the boundary of the area. By calculating the proportion of points that lie inside the area, we can estimate the area enclosed by the curve.
``` {r task2.2}
generate_random_points <- function(n_samples) {
  if (!is.numeric(n_samples) || n_samples <= 0) {
    stop("Error: n_samples must be a positive integer.")
  }
  
  x_random <- tryCatch({
    runif(n_samples, -2, 3.5)
  }, error = function(e) {
    stop("Error generating x_random: ", e$message)
  })
  
  y_random <- tryCatch({
    runif(n_samples, -3, 3)
  }, error = function(e) {
    stop("Error generating y_random: ", e$message)
  })
  
  inside_area <- tryCatch({
    as.integer(sapply(1:n_samples, function(i) {
      distance_from_origin <- sqrt(x_random[i]^2 + y_random[i]^2)
      distance_from_origin <= r_function(atan2(y_random[i], x_random[i]))
    }))
  }, error = function(e) {
    stop("Error calculating inside_area: ", e$message)
  })
  
  if (length(inside_area) != n_samples) {
    stop("Error: The number of inside_area values does not match n_samples.")
  }
  
  data.frame(x = x_random, y = y_random, inside = inside_area)
}

n_samples <- 1000
random_points <- tryCatch({
  generate_random_points(n_samples)
}, error = function(e) {
  stop("Error generating random points: ", e$message)
})

t_vals <- seq(-pi, pi, length.out = 1000)
polar_coords <- tryCatch({
  t(sapply(t_vals, polar_to_cartesian))
}, error = function(e) {
  stop("Error in converting polar to Cartesian coordinates: ", e$message)
})

x_vals <- polar_coords[, 1]
y_vals <- polar_coords[, 2]

ggplot() +
  geom_path(aes(x = x_vals, y = y_vals), color = "blue", size = 1) +
  geom_point(data = random_points, aes(x = x, y = y, color = factor(inside)), size = 0.5) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("red", "green"), labels = c("Outside", "Inside")) +
  coord_fixed() +
  theme_minimal() +
  labs(title = "Random Points Inside/Outside the Enclosed Area",
       x = "X", y = "Y", color = "Point Status") +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```

